# .copy-allow — lines that NAME a banned word in order to promise its absence.
#
# A gate that bans a word cannot scan the copy that says the word is absent.
# This app already met that on `src/report/releases.ts`, where the release note
# whose job was to say there are no streaks here had to say "streaks"; that one
# is held to a different rule set. This file is the same problem in ordinary
# screen copy, and it is a LIST rather than a pattern for the reason the hub's
# `.quote-allow` is (LESSONS 108): honest prose and the thing being forbidden
# are the same shape, so a rule that tries to tell them apart flags the app's
# own voice.
#
# One line per declaration, `path | the exact line, trimmed`. A declaration that
# matches nothing FAILS, so rewriting a sentence cannot leave its cover behind.
# Never declare a line that actually does the banned thing — the point of the
# entry is that the sentence PROMISES the absence.

public/index.html | <p>Pick one. Five questions, one step at a time. Nothing is recorded and nothing is scored.</p>
